cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "When Routing Entropy Tracks Length, Not Complexity: A Cross-Model Token-Position Confound in MoE Interpretability"
abstract: >-
  Mixture-of-experts (MoE) language models route each token to a small subset of
  experts, and the entropy of that routing distribution is a convenient
  one-number summary that a higher value invites reading as a sign the model is
  working harder. Across a 168-prompt suite graded into twelve levels of intended
  cognitive complexity, routing entropy averaged over all prefill tokens rises
  with the intended level in two MoE models, yet the same averaged quantity
  tracks prompt token count at least as strongly. Routing entropy rises with
  token position inside the prefill, so longer prompts collect more high-entropy
  late positions and lift the all-token mean; read at the final prefill token the
  gradient disappears and the extreme levels reverse. The aggregate gradient
  reflects token count and token position, so routing entropy compared across
  prompts of different lengths should be read at a position-controlled readout.
